A||||23||ChadMan||The case of the stumbling Duke||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||02-09-2001||10:58 AM||||What would you do if this were your car?  My Coupe has three minor problems:<P>1)  If you run the engine at 2100-2500 under light or no load, it sets a trouble code 44 (lean exhaust).<BR>2)  At idle, the rpms sometimes drop to 400 (near-stall), at which point the IAC opens, the car revs to 1300, then settles back to 1000.<BR>3)  At wide-open throttle, the car usually stumbles at high rpm (4000 and up).<P>Now here's the kicker:  the car has had these problems since it rolled off the dealer's lot in spring 1988.  I have receipts from the original owner - he took it back to the dealer with only 2,800 miles on it complaining of these symptoms.  Dennis Pontiac replaced a bunch of sensors, the coilpaks and the PROM, but couldn't solve the problem.  He then moved to Florida, where the local Pontiac dealer verified all electricals were working fine, diagnosed "internal vacuum leak through cylinder head," (???) and recommended sending the head to a machine shop.  By now the car had 12,500 miles on it and the dealer said the guy would have to pay for the repair himself (bullcrap!), so he didn't have it done.<P>Fast-forward to 2001.  From a reliability standpoint, this has been the best car I have ever owned.  But it still has these annoying minor problems that keep me from enjoying it like I should.<P>I have tested and/or replaced every sensor on this car.  I have replaced the intake manifold gasket and all vacuum lines, and rebuilt the TBI (new gaskets and injector).  I have replaced the ECM and PROM with a known-good unit from Ed Parks.  But it keeps on stumbling.  Neither of my mechanics could fix it.<P>I would have replaced the cylinder head before now, but compression is good - a low of 150 PSI on cylinder #2, a high of 160 PSI on cylinder #1.  I'm not afraid to replace the head, but I'd hate to invest the time and money and not have it fix the problem.<P>What would you do if it were YOUR rust-free, never-wrecked, nice-looking, brand-new-brakes-having Coupe?||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||02-09-2001||11:00 AM||||And please don't suggest "Just drive it as it is" -- I'd do that if it were a Chevette, but it's a Fiero and I want to autocross it and enjoy it as a sports car!||||reg||12
Z||000002||MontanaFiero||02-09-2001||01:46 PM||||What year car is this, anyway?<BR>I would pull the head off and have it pressure tested. IF it is good, then I would have it planed in case of warpage. Pressure testing doesn't cost that much, but if the Pontiac dealer diagnosed a vacuum leak in the head, and nothing else fixes the problem, then I'd say that's what it is.  Easy stuff.<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neverfinished||02-09-2001||02:46 PM||||TPS???<BR>i just replaced mine and made my car run smooooooooooooooooooooth as glass||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||02-09-2001||03:22 PM||||It's an 88 Coupe.<P>Montana, that's a good suggestion.  If I pull the head, I will have to replace the head gasket, exhaust manifold gasket, intake manifold gasket, and head-to-block bolts, right?  If I do all that, I'm tempted to spend the extra $250 or so and buy a reman head.<P>Good suggestion, Neverfinished, but I already replaced the TPS.  And the MAP sensor.  And the 02 sensor.  And the coolant temperature sensor.  And the DIS brick...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Thanks, guys.  Please keep brainstorming and keep the ideas coming...I want to go autocrossing this year and try to kick some Civic butt in E Stock!<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||02-09-2001||04:22 PM||||have you checked the fuel pressure?<BR>new fuel filter<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||02-09-2001||04:26 PM||||Thanks, fierospeeder!  But my mechanic thought of that in 1997 - he replaced the fuel pump and filter, but it didn't fix it.  At least I have a spare fuel pump now...<P>Keep 'em coming!||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||02-09-2001||04:57 PM||||The first thing that comes to mind that I didn't see mentioned is the EGR valve. A faulty one or the wrong one could cause these symptoms. Also, at least on the older 4 cyl, it MUST be connected to the correct vacuum port on the throttle body.<P>I'm not sure how a head gets an internal vacuum leak... a crack in the intake runner???<P>If all else fails... Sounds like a good excuse for a motor conversion...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-09-2001||08:09 PM||||Go thru the paper trail and try to find exactly what sensors have been replaced and give us the list. Of interest to me are MAP and MAT. MAT especially can be screwed up enough to cause a problem but still test good. <P>If either of those is screwed up it could do this. check over the MAP and PCV vac lines very carefully. GM recomends clamping all joints on the PCV line. Be gentle or the clamps will cut the elbows.<P>You didn't mention if EGR was replaced. Take it off and replace it's gasket. any leak there could cause problems. If the ERG is iffy replace it.<P>Also have to check the brake booster. Disconnnect the booster line from the intake and plug the port.<P>Read the article in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||02-10-2001||09:51 AM||||Now we may be getting somewhere.  I replaced the EGR gasket when I replaced the intake manifold gasket, and I'm sure the EGR vacuum line is properly connected and free of leaks.  But I didn't replace the EGR valve itself -- I thought it was working OK, but that was 4 years ago and when I think back, I think all I did to test it was make sure it could be moved by hand (the one on my 84 was frozen).  How should I test the EGR valve?<P>I know I replaced both the MAP and MAT sensors and the PCV valve.  Both ends of the PCV tube are clamped and appear to be in good shape, though the metal line has a kink in it where some dummy (not me -- some other, previous dummy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) tried to change the PCV valve without unbolting the metal line from the intake manifold first.  Doesn't look like it's leaking though.<P>I have never checked the brake booster and will do that today.  For some reason, I can't get the Ogre cave to load -- my browser can find it, but I get a blank page.<P>Thanks, everybody!  More ideas welcome...||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||02-10-2001||01:40 PM||||Each PCV elbow should have 2 clamps. That kink is a bad thing. take the line off and check it really carefully. with the car running spray some cleaner on it and make certain it's not pinholed.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Indiana_resto_guy||02-10-2001||01:52 PM||||Testing the EGR valve requires that suction be applied, it should hold that same value of vacumn. Usually test mine for a couple of minutes.<BR>The brake booster, start engine and let it build negative pressure and shut off enging. <BR>Depress pedal and hold for a second or two and listen. If you can hear any air venting (a small hiss) you may have a pin hole. Repeat process If you don't hear anything and you should be able to get around 3 good pumps befor the pedal is hard to depress.<BR>I doubt the booster is bad judging the time frame of the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000012||2M4 Dale||02-10-2001||07:17 PM||||Chad...have you had your catalytic convertor checked...It could be pluged up......If you can't breath then you cough...same with your Fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by 2M4 Dale (edited 02-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChadMan||02-12-2001||08:45 AM||||Well, the brake booster seems fine.  Haven't had a chance to check the EGR valve operation yet.<P>I thought of the cat too, Dale.  So I replaced the cat with a straight pipe, and then replaced the straight pipe with a new cat from the Fiero Store.  It didn't fix the stumbling problem, but it did pick up a little pep when I got rid of the original cat.  Luckily, it seems to run just as good with the Fiero Store cat as it did with the straight pipe!||||reg||1
Z||000014||william_thorniii||02-12-2001||09:08 AM||||Chad,<P>Have you run the car with an OTC moniter plugged in?  If so, when the car would stumble did you notice any strange readings from any of the sensors?  If you would like to try it, lmk because I would not mind loaning you my OTC II for a while.  I also have a vacuum gauge that you can test your EGR with.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000015||ChadMan||02-12-2001||03:13 PM||||I took the car to a buddy who works at a local service station.  He put it on a diagnostic computer, but said we couldn't get accurate readings unless the idle would say below 900 rpm, and it wouldn't come down below 1000.  I can still see him cupping his hands over the intake trying to get the idle to come down!<P>I'd love to try your monitor, William, but would feel funny borrowing a valuable piece of equipment from you -- plus I'd like to see your reaction to what the monitor said.  Maybe we could get together sometime and test it?  I could pay you in pizza and beer.  This might be a good excuse to go to 87ECO5's place and check out his Coupe when it comes back from engine-transplant surgery.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||JOHN R. ALLEN III||02-12-2001||03:44 PM||||CHAD... REPLACE THE EGR.  MY 1986 HAD THE SAME PROBLEM!  I REPLACED EVEERYTHING FEELING LIKE THE EGR WOULD NEVER BE THE PROBLEM AND NO TECH MANUALS STATED IT COULD BE... IT WAS!||||reg||1
Z||000017||ChadMan||02-12-2001||04:26 PM||||John, you have (or had) an 86 4-banger that was doing the same thing mine is?  It wouldn't kill me to replace the EGR, because it's only $65 from wrenchead.com.  And that is one thing I didn't really check out throughly - when I acertained that the EGR valve wasn't frozen and the vacuum line was good, I forgot about it.<P>I'm just skeptical because the EGR valve on my 84 Coupe was frozen partway open and it still ran fine.||||reg||5
Z||000018||JOHN R. ALLEN III||02-12-2001||04:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>John, you have (or had) an 86 4-banger that was doing the same thing mine is?  It wouldn't kill me to replace the EGR, because it's only $65 from wrenchead.com.  And that is one thing I didn't really check out throughly - when I acertained that the EGR valve wasn't frozen and the vacuum line was good, I forgot about it.<P>I'm just skeptical because the EGR valve on my 84 Coupe was frozen partway open and it still ran fine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||JOHN R. ALLEN III||02-12-2001||04:56 PM||||CHAD... MINE WAS NOT STUCK EITHER... WHAT THEY DO (WHEN FAULTY)IS "FLUCTUATE" IN OPENING AND THERE FOR CAUSE THE ERRATIC IDLE, PARTICULARLY WITH LOW LOAD||||reg||1
Z||000020||ChadMan||02-13-2001||08:32 AM||||For the cost of the EGR valve and gasket, I guess I'd be crazy not to replace them - though I won't get to it until 2/24.<P>Thanks to everybody for their thoughts on this.  John, Ogre...if the EGR valve fixes my problem I am going to be really grateful.||||reg||1
Z||000021||JOHN R. ALLEN III||02-13-2001||09:02 AM||||HEY I REALLY THINK IT WILL DO THE TRICK.  I TESTED, REPLACED, CURSED, CALLED THE CAR JUNK, THREATENED TO IGNITE SAME, ETC., UNTIL I FINALLY REPLACED THE EGR AS A LAST "GUESS" EFFORT.  THE THING THAT REALLY DISTURBED ME IS NO MANUAL REFERENCED REPLACEMENT AS A CLEAR SOLUTION... BY THE WAY MY EGR COST WAS AROUND $45-$50 AT "ADVANCED AUTO" A LARGE PARTS FRANCHISE... PLEASE E-MAIL ME AND LET ME KNOW HOW YOU MAKE OUT||||reg||1
Z||000022||CHUCKVH||02-14-2001||12:43 AM||||I feel your pain!!  I went through this exact problem with my 88 2.5 coupe last summer.  I spent lots of money, replaced lots of parts, bought a scan tool, practically lived in the factory shop manual, etc.  It turned out to be a number of hairline cracks in the exhaust manifold, in the vicinity of the oxygen sensor.  The manual tells you to check for exhaust leaks, and I did, several times.  I finally spotted the cracks when I hung upside down with a mirror and flashlight.  When I released the torque on the manifold, the cracks really opened up.  The new manifold I got from GM had improperly welded flanges, so I had to do a lot of machining to get a good seal to the head.  By the way, the EGR on the 88 WILL NOT hold a vacuum, it is designed to operate using manifold vacuum and exhaust pressure.  As I recall, the test is to rev the engine and check for diaphragm movement.  Sitting at the curb, you will get very little movement, and it can be hard to detect.  I usually use a mirror and flashlight, though sometimes you can feel it move with a fingertip if you are careful.||||reg||1
Z||000023||ChadMan||02-14-2001||09:02 AM||||I can't find any receipts for the EGR valve having ever been replaced, so for the $65 or so, I plan to go ahead and replace it Saturday 2/24 - it's 14 years and 118K miles old, so I don't feel like I'm throwing money away.<P>I looked the exhaust manifold over pretty well when I replaced the O2 sensor a couple of years ago and didn't notice anything wrong with it.  Maybe this would be a good excuse to get the header like SloPonti has.||||reg||1
